    {
      "id": "5c5daf3f-137a-4eea-8133-5d6be9739db1",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# LinkedHashMap – properties and methods\r\n\r\n`LinkedHashMap` is a `HashMap` that **remembers the insertion order** of its keys.\r\n\r\n**Properties**\r\n- Unique keys, allows one `null` key.\r\n- Pairs come out in the **order you added them** (updating a value with `put` does not change the order).\r\n- Can also keep **access order** (most recently used last) with `new LinkedHashMap<>(16, 0.75f, true)`, which is how a simple LRU cache is built.\r\n- Since **Java 21** it also has methods to work with both ends: `firstEntry`, `lastEntry`, `putFirst`, `putLast`, `pollFirstEntry`, `pollLastEntry`, `reversed`.",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "5c5daf3f-137a-4eea-8133-5d6be9739db1"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "af31c8bf-5b21-4c12-9468-9caa52b479f2",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "//Syntax\r\n//Map<KeyType, ValueType> variable_name = new LinkedHashMap<KeyType, ValueType>();\r\n\r\n//this is to show the order difference between HashMap and LinkedHashMap\r\n// Method: new LinkedHashMap<>()\r\n// What:   creates an empty map that keeps the insertion order of its keys\r\n// Use:    when you need key → value pairs in the order you added them\r\nMap<String, Integer> linked = new LinkedHashMap<String, Integer>();\r\nMap<String, Integer> hash = new HashMap<String, Integer>();\r\n\r\nfor (String city : List.of(\"Pune\", \"Mumbai\", \"Goa\", \"Delhi\")) {\r\n    // Method: put(key, value)\r\n    // What:   adds the pair (here: city → length of its name)\r\n    // Use:    to fill both maps with the same pairs\r\n    linked.put(city, city.length());\r\n    hash.put(city, city.length());\r\n}\r\n\r\nSystem.out.println(\"LinkedHashMap: \" + linked);\r\nSystem.out.println(\"HashMap:       \" + hash);",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "af31c8bf-5b21-4c12-9468-9caa52b479f2",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 16,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 16,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "LinkedHashMap: {Pune=4, Mumbai=6, Goa=3, Delhi=5}\r\nHashMap:       {Delhi=5, Goa=3, Pune=4, Mumbai=6}\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 16
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "d3a8673b-ee85-4629-9b38-29cdf126e973",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "//this is to keep the ACCESS order instead of insertion order\r\n// Method: new LinkedHashMap<>(initialCapacity, loadFactor, accessOrder)\r\n// What:   with accessOrder = true, every get/put moves that key to the end (most recently used last)\r\n// Use:    to know which key was used least recently (the first one), e.g. for an LRU cache\r\nLinkedHashMap<String, Integer> recent = new LinkedHashMap<>(16, 0.75f, true);\r\nrecent.put(\"A\", 1);\r\nrecent.put(\"B\", 2);\r\nrecent.put(\"C\", 3);\r\nSystem.out.println(\"Before get: \" + recent);\r\n\r\nrecent.get(\"A\"); // A was just used, so it moves to the end\r\nSystem.out.println(\"After get(A): \" + recent);",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "d3a8673b-ee85-4629-9b38-29cdf126e973",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 17,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 17,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "Before get: {A=1, B=2, C=3}\r\nAfter get(A): {B=2, C=3, A=1}\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 17
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "bc448c1d-fb39-4040-8da6-6efc9b03dce4",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "LinkedHashMap<String, Integer> marks = new LinkedHashMap<>();\r\nmarks.put(\"Zoya\", 85);\r\nmarks.put(\"Jane\", 92);\r\nmarks.put(\"Doe\", 78);\r\n\r\n//Java 21+: methods to work with the first and last pairs\r\n// Method: firstEntry() / lastEntry()   [Java 21+]\r\n// What:   returns the first / last pair\r\n// Use:    to read the ends without a loop\r\nSystem.out.println(\"First: \" + marks.firstEntry() + \", Last: \" + marks.lastEntry());\r\n\r\n// Method: putFirst(key, value) / putLast(key, value)   [Java 21+]\r\n// What:   puts the pair at the start / end (moves it if the key already exists)\r\n// Use:    to control where a pair goes\r\nmarks.putFirst(\"Arbaz\", 88);\r\nmarks.putLast(\"Zoya\", 90);\r\nSystem.out.println(\"After putFirst/putLast: \" + marks);\r\n\r\n// Method: pollFirstEntry() / pollLastEntry()   [Java 21+]\r\n// What:   removes and returns the first / last pair\r\n// Use:    to take pairs from either end\r\nSystem.out.println(\"pollFirstEntry: \" + marks.pollFirstEntry());\r\nSystem.out.println(\"The map is: \" + marks);\r\n\r\n// Method: reversed()   [Java 21+]\r\n// What:   returns a reversed view of the map\r\n// Use:    to read the map from last to first\r\nSystem.out.println(\"Reversed: \" + marks.reversed());",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "bc448c1d-fb39-4040-8da6-6efc9b03dce4",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 18,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 18,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "First: Zoya=85, Last: Doe=78\r\nAfter putFirst/putLast: {Arbaz=88, Jane=92, Doe=78, Zoya=90}\r\npollFirstEntry: Arbaz=88\r\nThe map is: {Jane=92, Doe=78, Zoya=90}\r\nReversed: {Zoya=90, Doe=78, Jane=92}\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 18
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "87810131-7a87-4d11-9358-35d175530f06",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# TreeMap – properties and methods\r\n\r\n`TreeMap` keeps its pairs **sorted by key** (numbers small → big, text A → Z).\r\n\r\n**Properties**\r\n- Unique keys, **no `null` key** (values can be `null`).\r\n- Always sorted by key, in natural order or by a `Comparator` you give it.\r\n- Slower than `HashMap` (`put`, `get`, `remove` take `O(log n)`), but gives you extra methods to search by key order: `firstKey`, `lastKey`, `floorKey`, `ceilingKey`, `headMap`, `tailMap`, …\r\n- Declare it as `TreeMap<...>` (or `NavigableMap<...>`) to use those extra methods.",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "87810131-7a87-4d11-9358-35d175530f06"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "f7fd8312-fbdc-41ee-a7f9-e10daff01575",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "//Syntax\r\n//TreeMap<KeyType, ValueType> variable_name = new TreeMap<KeyType, ValueType>();\r\n\r\n//this is to create a map sorted by key\r\n// Method: new TreeMap<>()\r\n// What:   creates an empty map that keeps its keys sorted\r\n// Use:    when you need the pairs in key order\r\nTreeMap<Integer, String> ranks = new TreeMap<Integer, String>();\r\nranks.put(3, \"Doe\");\r\nranks.put(1, \"Zoya\");\r\nranks.put(4, \"Arbaz\");\r\nranks.put(2, \"Jane\");\r\nSystem.out.println(\"The map is: \" + ranks);\r\n\r\n// Method: firstKey() / lastKey()\r\n// What:   returns the smallest / biggest key\r\n// Use:    to get the minimum and maximum key quickly\r\nSystem.out.println(\"firstKey: \" + ranks.firstKey() + \", lastKey: \" + ranks.lastKey());\r\n\r\n// Method: firstEntry() / lastEntry()\r\n// What:   returns the pair with the smallest / biggest key\r\n// Use:    to get both the key and the value at the ends\r\nSystem.out.println(\"firstEntry: \" + ranks.firstEntry() + \", lastEntry: \" + ranks.lastEntry());",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "f7fd8312-fbdc-41ee-a7f9-e10daff01575",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 19,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 19,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "The map is: {1=Zoya, 2=Jane, 3=Doe, 4=Arbaz}\r\nfirstKey: 1, lastKey: 4\r\nfirstEntry: 1=Zoya, lastEntry: 4=Arbaz\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 19
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "79f1027c-6540-4e2c-9288-3660986b6bc8",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "TreeMap<Integer, String> grades = new TreeMap<>();\r\ngrades.put(0, \"F\");\r\ngrades.put(40, \"D\");\r\ngrades.put(60, \"C\");\r\ngrades.put(75, \"B\");\r\ngrades.put(90, \"A\");\r\n\r\n//this is to find the closest keys to a number\r\n// Method: floorKey(x) / ceilingKey(x)\r\n// What:   biggest key <= x / smallest key >= x (null if none)\r\n// Use:    to find the nearest key, including x itself\r\nSystem.out.println(\"floorKey(68): \" + grades.floorKey(68));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"ceilingKey(68): \" + grades.ceilingKey(68));\r\n\r\n// Method: lowerKey(x) / higherKey(x)\r\n// What:   biggest key < x / smallest key > x (x itself is never returned)\r\n// Use:    to find the key just before / just after\r\nSystem.out.println(\"lowerKey(60): \" + grades.lowerKey(60));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"higherKey(60): \" + grades.higherKey(60));\r\n\r\n// Method: floorEntry(x)\r\n// What:   returns the pair with the biggest key <= x\r\n// Use:    a range lookup, here: turn marks into a grade\r\nSystem.out.println(\"Marks 68 -> grade \" + grades.floorEntry(68).getValue());\r\nSystem.out.println(\"Marks 95 -> grade \" + grades.floorEntry(95).getValue());",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "79f1027c-6540-4e2c-9288-3660986b6bc8",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 20,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 20,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "floorKey(68): 60\r\nceilingKey(68): 75\r\nlowerKey(60): 40\r\nhigherKey(60): 75\r\nMarks 68 -> grade C\r\nMarks 95 -> grade A\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 20
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "6a666727-c933-417a-bf99-ee005da0b865",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "TreeMap<Integer, String> ranks = new TreeMap<>(Map.of(1, \"Zoya\", 2, \"Jane\", 3, \"Doe\", 4, \"Arbaz\", 5, \"John\"));\r\n\r\n//this is to get a part of the map\r\n// Method: headMap(to)\r\n// What:   pairs with keys smaller than \"to\" (to is excluded)\r\n// Use:    to get everything below a key\r\nSystem.out.println(\"headMap(3): \" + ranks.headMap(3));\r\n\r\n// Method: tailMap(from)\r\n// What:   pairs with keys from \"from\" upwards (from is included)\r\n// Use:    to get everything from a key up\r\nSystem.out.println(\"tailMap(3): \" + ranks.tailMap(3));\r\n\r\n// Method: subMap(from, to)\r\n// What:   pairs with keys from \"from\" (included) to \"to\" (excluded)\r\n// Use:    to get a range of keys\r\nSystem.out.println(\"subMap(2, 4): \" + ranks.subMap(2, 4));",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "6a666727-c933-417a-bf99-ee005da0b865",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 21,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 21,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "headMap(3): {1=Zoya, 2=Jane}\r\ntailMap(3): {3=Doe, 4=Arbaz, 5=John}\r\nsubMap(2, 4): {2=Jane, 3=Doe}\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 21
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "48f9486a-9f7b-4248-ae73-8e1d78f858ce",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "TreeMap<Integer, String> ranks = new TreeMap<>(Map.of(1, \"Zoya\", 2, \"Jane\", 3, \"Doe\"));\r\n\r\n//this is to read the map from biggest key to smallest\r\n// Method: descendingMap()\r\n// What:   returns a reversed (big → small) view of the map\r\n// Use:    to show pairs in descending key order\r\nSystem.out.println(\"Descending: \" + ranks.descendingMap());\r\n\r\n// Method: descendingKeySet()\r\n// What:   returns the keys from biggest to smallest\r\n// Use:    when you only need the keys in reverse\r\nSystem.out.println(\"Descending keys: \" + ranks.descendingKeySet());\r\n\r\n//this is to take out the first and last pair\r\n// Method: pollFirstEntry() / pollLastEntry()\r\n// What:   removes and returns the pair with the smallest / biggest key (null if empty)\r\n// Use:    to process pairs in key order, one at a time\r\nSystem.out.println(\"pollFirstEntry: \" + ranks.pollFirstEntry());\r\nSystem.out.println(\"pollLastEntry: \" + ranks.pollLastEntry());\r\nSystem.out.println(\"The map is: \" + ranks);",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "48f9486a-9f7b-4248-ae73-8e1d78f858ce",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 22,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 22,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "Descending: {3=Doe, 2=Jane, 1=Zoya}\r\nDescending keys: [3, 2, 1]\r\npollFirstEntry: 1=Zoya\r\npollLastEntry: 3=Doe\r\nThe map is: {2=Jane}\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 22
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ae423d16-7941-4104-9ecc-e6fd474cc6cc",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "//this is to sort the keys in a different order with a Comparator\r\n// Method: new TreeMap<>(Comparator.reverseOrder())\r\n// What:   creates a TreeMap that sorts keys big → small (Z → A for text)\r\n// Use:    when the natural order of the keys is not the one you need\r\nTreeMap<String, Integer> marks = new TreeMap<>(Comparator.reverseOrder());\r\nmarks.putAll(Map.of(\"Zoya\", 85, \"Jane\", 92, \"Doe\", 78, \"Arbaz\", 88));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"Z to A: \" + marks);\r\n\r\n//marks.put(null, 50);   // not allowed: TreeMap cannot have a null key (the program stops with an error)",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "ae423d16-7941-4104-9ecc-e6fd474cc6cc",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 23,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 23,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "Z to A: {Zoya=85, Jane=92, Doe=78, Arbaz=88}\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 23
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cbfe8275-718c-4fa8-bc5b-620b8891e374",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# HashMap vs LinkedHashMap vs TreeMap vs Hashtable\r\n\r\n| | `HashMap` | `LinkedHashMap` | `TreeMap` | `Hashtable` |\r\n|---|---|---|---|---|\r\n| Order of keys | None | Insertion (or access) order | Sorted | None |\r\n| Duplicate keys | ❌ (value is replaced) | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ |\r\n| `null` key / values | ✅ one / ✅ | ✅ one / ✅ | ❌ / ✅ | ❌ / ❌ |\r\n| Speed of `put` / `get` | Fastest `O(1)` | Fast `O(1)` | Slower `O(log n)` | Slower (locking) |\r\n| Thread-safe | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ (legacy, like `Vector`) |\r\n| Extra methods | – | `firstEntry`, `putFirst`, `reversed`… (Java 21+) | `firstKey`, `floorKey`, `headMap`, `tailMap`, `pollFirstEntry`… | – |\r\n| Use when | Default choice | You need the order you added | You need keys sorted | Old code only (use `ConcurrentHashMap` for threads) |",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "cbfe8275-718c-4fa8-bc5b-620b8891e374"
      }
    }
  ]
}